                "doc.tuning.step1_title": "Listen to the Motor Pitch:",
                "doc.tuning.step1_body": "A healthy rotary motor hums with a steady frequency. If the motor sags, sputters, or drops pitch when entering skin, it is struggling against cartridge membrane resistance or needle friction. If it emits a high-pitched whine, it is over-revving.",
                "doc.tuning.step2_title": "Inspect the Line & Deposit:",
                "doc.tuning.step2_body": "Linework should deposit smoothly on a steady continuous pass. If the needle skips or snags, consider whether stroke length or voltage requires a 0.2V-0.4V upward adjustment. For shading, look for controlled stippling rather than cutting.",
                "doc.tuning.step3_title": "Adjust in Small Increments:",
                "doc.tuning.step3_body": "Never adjust by full volts. Tune in 0.2V to 0.5V steps and make multiple passes before re-adjusting.",
                "doc.coil.title": "Coil Machine Tuning Order",
                "doc.coil.intro": "On traditional electromagnetic coil machines, voltage only adjusts the strength of the magnetic field in the coil cores. The actual operating frequency and hit character are dictated by mechanical geometry:",
